      "title": "Nielsen Norman Group outlines UX role shifts in agentic software factories",
      "summary": "Nielsen Norman Group reports that AI-native software development lifecycles reduce traditional UX to AI context, requiring user experience teams to actively discover needs, shape what is built, and evaluate output before and after launch.",
      "key_facts": [
        "AI-native software development lifecycles reduce UX to AI context",
        "UX must discover user needs, shape builds, and evaluate outcomes",
        "Published by Nielsen Norman Group"
      ],
      "design_implication": "When development workflows reduce interface design specifications into raw AI context files, design teams risk losing direct control over component semantics and interaction flows. Shifting toward agentic software production requires designers to evolve from drawing static screens to curating the contextual boundaries and intent constraints consumed by coding agents. Start by auditing your team's design system documentation to ensure structural rules are explicit enough for an automated agent to interpret accurately.",
      "implication_lens": "workflow",
      "visual_need": "none",
      "source_name": "Nielsen Norman Group",
